      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking on asphalt pavements, such as you've noticed in Phoenix, can often be attributed to thermal stresses. These stresses arise due to the a/c cycle—that is, the pavement expanding during warm temperatures and contracting as it cools, which can be quite extreme in Phoenix's climate. Bitumen, which acts as a binder in asphalt, can be affected by high temperatures and UV exposure, leading to softening and potentially reducing its effectiveness in maintaining pavement integrity. Insufficient aggregate sizes or poor-quality materials can also contribute to these cracking issues.",
