    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. We also suspect it might be related to the subgrade. Can you expand on how subgrade compaction or moisture issues underneath the asphalt might play into these longitudinal cracks? I want to see if we're looking at a material issue or a foundation problem.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subgrade conditions are critical for the structural support of asphalt pavements. Inadequate compaction or unsuitable soil types can create a weak foundation that is prone to settlement and cracking as traffic loads and thermal expansion occur. Moisture issues, whether from poor drainage or excessive saturation, place additional stress on the pavement as water can swell the subgrade soil, leading to loss of load-bearing capacity. Addressing these subgrade problems might involve reassessment of compaction methods, soil testing, and improved drainage, which could prevent or mitigate longitudinal cracking in your asphalt.",
